good morning everybody I have enormous  pleasure and honor in speaking to you  this morning as part of this important  conference in starting the morning  looking back at the history of culture  in crisis on the Korean Peninsula I feel  that we have millennia of time and no  time to consider and to think about I  have four topics which I hope to  interest you in in the short time  available to me I am fortunate to have  visited both Korea's in the course of my  work at the Victoria and Albert Museum  and in the British British Library and  I'm particularly interested to think of  Korea as a site for world culture for  cultural transmission and for lessons  for all of humanity about what happens  when conflict visits in unexpected ways  Korea as you see is surrounded by China  by Russia and by Japan we will return to  this in geopolitical environment in a  moment but I want to begin by looking at  an object which is not Korean an object  which is Chinese a famous lacquer  Blatter basket which was excavated in  the early 20th century when Korea was a  colony of Japan this basket has row upon  row of amazing 2,000 year old paintings  depicting people who honor their parents  honoring your parents is a human  characteristic it's a center point of  east asian Confucian civilization this  is the most potent memory of that long  tradition in Korea and in China it comes  to be in Korea because in the first  century AD parts of Korea had been  colonized by Han China  after the mid 20th century this object  is hardly known outside of Korea because  it is cut off by the accident of world  politics and it becomes part of the  collection of the North Korean National  Museum not the not the more open South  Korean national museum I'm fortunate to  have seen it myself I'm also concerned  to know where it might be now and I  think it's an object that just tells us  so much about how culture expresses  identity and culture crosses borders  that's my first Korean a lesson of  culture in crisis my second point from  Korea is about place religion and the  spread of faith from continent to  continent Korea is a site of wonderful  Buddhist temples many of which were  scattered across a beautiful senic area  the diamond mountains which spans the  eastern coast of the Korean Peninsula it  was much painted by the scholars of the  eighteenth and nineteenth centuries it's  still the subject of inspiration to  artists such as mr. Hwang in Jay who is  a eminent North Korean print artists  represented in our collection here sadly  during the korean war between 1950 and  1953 all the buddhist temples in the  Diamond Mountain area came to  destruction a huge heritage of human  civilization and belief was lost as a  protracted war and negotiation to and  negotiations about the fate of prisoners  of war dragged out across three years  carpet bombing continued across North  Korea until as one scholar has said  there was nothing left to bomb and at  that time all of the temples  we're lost here is sock one temple the  temple of the Buddha King as it was in  the Japanese period here it is in 1910  and here it is today one date one gate  we alone surviving other buildings are  nothing but architectural plans today  many temples in that eastern part of  Korea were lost in similar ways here is  a second temple senior temple all that  is left of what was once a large and  impressive agglomeration of buildings is  this one pagoda dating to the 6th  century AD it's easy to forget that such  destruct destruction accompanies almost  every conflict that we know of my third  point is about the accidents of history  that are visited upon unknowing  populations I'm turning now to the mid  19th century when Korea then known as  the Hermit Kingdom a resolutely closed  society which treasured its status as a  place apart with little contact outside  its own borders when that country came  into conflict with the forces of  expansionist colonial powers here we see  in the 1870s American marines attacking  con qua island which is near the court  the capital Seoul here we see battles on  the same island these skirmishes these  attacks on the Korean state went on for  decades in the 1880s we the British set  up a garrison in the island of komando  off the south of the Korean coast naming  it port Hamilton and setting up our own  station there we send warships we  we remain there for a couple of years  and then we moved on ready to engage in  other parts of East Asia this enforced  contact with the military might of the  Western world led to the militarization  of Korean people themselves we have  troops here being being trained and we  have the evidence of photography in  these 19th century periods to help us  trace the story we have all the  accoutrements of war as we've come to  know it hospitals refugees and we have  prisoners and border conflict moving  along my final reflections on conflict  and cultural heritage in Korea take us  to today and to the role of museums in  the presentation of cultural identity  the modern states of Korea are of course  to the Democratic People's Republic in  the north and the Republic in the south  each has a National Museum each has a  narrative each has a taxis value to  cultural heritage my message I hope is  one that sees some optimism for culture  over time as a unifying and  strengthening element in public life  both tangible cultural heritage of the  kind that we here in the museum world  are so concerned with and other cultural  heritage of a literary or performative  nature matter deeply to people all  around the world and that's the case in  both Korea's although this Stalinist  building of the centre of Pyongyang the  Korean Central history museum is a place  that is far in its museological practice  from the norms of the international  community it is nonetheless a portent  statement of the importance of culture  to the neck to the matter to the nation  and in the South 21st century  architecture technological achievement  and a much more open society have led to  a fine National Museum in the southern  part of soul which I imagine many in  this room will have will have visited  and we are able to see here the interior  of the National Museum in the south of  Korea in the Republic not forbidden to  photographers in the way that its  counterpart in the north is I wish the  conference success and look forward to  making many comparisons in the course of  the day thank you
 

